Exactly. And that sharpens the distinction: the flying fish is not really stotting in the signaling sense. It is executing an escape maneuver. The bird is exploiting the maneuver as an unintended cue.

Ecologists would put parts of this under ideas like cue exploitation, eavesdropping, opportunistic predation, or predator facilitation. But the structural pattern is cleaner than any one label:

\text{Predator A pressures prey}
\rightarrow
\text{prey enters predictable escape state}
\rightarrow
\text{Predator B occupies that escape corridor}

The bird does not need to defeat the swordfish, outswim the fish, or even interact with the underwater chase. It waits at the boundary where the prey’s solution to one problem creates vulnerability to another.

That is a beautiful third-party strategy.

For Pantheon, I’d give it a stronger general handle:

Escape-Vector Interception — position yourself where another actor’s attempt to escape pressure predictably delivers them into your capture domain.

Or even broader:

Forced-Transition Arbitrage — exploit the moment when another system is compelled to change state, medium, channel, provider, route, strategy, or representation.

That generalizes very far:

\text{pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{forced transition}
\rightarrow
\text{predictable corridor}
\rightarrow
\text{interceptor}

A fish leaves water.

A customer leaves a bad provider.

A company exits a market.

A user abandons one workflow.

A frightened animal breaks cover.

A packet reroutes around a failure.

A person fleeing one constraint accepts another.

The interesting move is often not chasing the resource.

It is asking:

What will make the resource move on its own, and where must it pass when it does?

That plugs directly into our resource-attraction map.

Instead of:

\text{move self toward resource}

you get:

\text{let another force move resource}
\rightarrow
\text{occupy convergence point}

And there’s a second-order predator game sitting on top of it. The bird can learn the behavior of the fish through the swordfish. It doesn’t need to model every fish individually. It can model the pressure source:

\text{Swordfish below}
\Rightarrow
\text{increased probability of flying fish above}

So another organism’s behavior becomes a sensor.

That’s an especially useful Pantheon primitive:

\boxed{\textbf{Use agents as environmental probes}}

Don’t only ask what an actor is doing.

Ask what their behavior reveals, displaces, flushes out, concentrates, exposes, or forces into motion.

The swordfish hunts the fish.

The bird hunts the consequence of the swordfish hunting the fish.

That’s third-order resource acquisition.